 <h1>Name of Exercise : Hollow Body Hold (with active core contraction)</h1>
<u><b><h4>Video Overview:</h4></b></u>
 The video features a 3D anatomical model lying on an exercise mat, performing a hollow body hold position.<br><br>The rectus abdominis (front abdominal wall) and core muscles are highlighted in yellow to show continuous, deep muscle engagement while the lower back stays flush against the<br><br> floor and the upper torso and legs remain elevated.

</div>
<pre>
 <h1>Step-by-Step How to Perform</h1> 
<b>1.Starting position:</b> Lie flat on your back on a mat with your legs extended together and your arms extended overhead or angled slightly out to the sides for balance.
<b>2.Engage the Core:</b>Press your lower back completely flat against the mat by tucking your pelvis slightly under (posterior pelvic tilt). There should be no space between your lower back and the floor.
<b>3.  Lift into Position: </b>Lift your feet, legs, shoulders, and upper back a few inches off the floor simultaneously.
            Keep your chin slightly tucked toward your chest without straining your neck.
<b>4. Hold & Breathe: </b>Maintain tension throughout your entire core, squeezing your glutes and quad muscles.
  Take short, controlled breaths while keeping your abdomen tight throughout the hold.
<b>5. Release:</b>  Gently lower your shoulders and legs back to the mat in a controlled manner once the time or rep count is complete.


                                                                                                                                                     
<h1>Benefits of Exercise</h1>

 <b>◉Core Stability & Strength:</b> Builds deep core strength across the entire abdominal wall (rectus abdominis, transverse abdominis, and obliques).
<b>◉Improved Gymnastic & Athletic Posture:</b> Teaches proper pelvic control, lower back flat position, and full-body tension essential for pull-ups, push-ups, handstands, and heavy compound lifts
<b>◉Reduces Lower Back Pain:</b> Helps correct excessive pelvic tilting (anterior pelvic tilt) by strengthening the anterior chain muscles.
<b>◉No Equipment Needed: </b> An effective bodyweight movement that can be done anywhere.


<h1>Sets, Reps & Hold Recommendations</h1> 

Because the Hollow Body Hold is an isometric hold (timed exercise) rather than a dynamic lifting movement, focus on time under tension rather than high rep counts.

| Fitness Level | Duration / Reps | Sets | Rest Between Sets |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Beginner | 15 – 20 seconds hold | 3 Sets | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Intermediate | 30 – 45 seconds hold | 3 – 4 Sets | 45 seconds |
| Advanced | 45 – 60+ seconds hold | 4 Sets | 30 – 45 seconds |

> Pro Tip: If your lower back arches off the mat at any point during the movement, bend your knees slightly or raise your legs higher until your lower back can stay pressed firmly against the ground.
